I spent an embarrassing amount of time debugging a memory leak that turned out to be an event listener I never removed. Once I started returning a cleanup function from every effect that subscribes to something, half my weird bugs disappeared overnight.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const handleResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
  window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);

  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
}, []);
```

If your effect subscribes, it should also unsubscribe. It's not glamorous but it'll save you a very confusing Friday.

Nobody wants to hit the API on every single keystroke, so here's the pattern I reach for whenever I need a search box that doesn't hammer the backend.

```jsx
function useDebouncedValue(value, delay = 400) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [value, delay]);

  return debounced;
}
```

Drop that in a search component, only fire the fetch when the debounced value changes, and your network tab will thank you.

It took me way too long to realize a custom hook is just a regular function that happens to call other hooks. That's it. That's the whole secret.

```jsx
function useToggle(initialValue = false) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue);
  const toggle = () => setValue((v) => !v);
  return [value, toggle];
}
```

Once that clicked for me I stopped being scared of writing my own and now I have about twelve of them scattered across every project I touch.

We drilled a single boolean through six components before someone finally said 'why don't we just use Context.' Here's roughly what that refactor looked like.

```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');

function App() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>
      <Dashboard />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Context isn't a replacement for real state management, but for stuff like theme or auth it's saved us from a lot of unnecessary prop chains.

I used to reach for a for loop by default until a coworker gently pointed out that half my loops were just map or filter in disguise.

```js
const prices = items.map((item) => item.price);
const inStock = items.filter((item) => item.quantity > 0);
const total = prices.reduce((sum, price) => sum + price, 0);
```

Now I barely write a raw for loop unless I actually need to break out early. My code reads more like a sentence now, which honestly still surprises me.

I mixed these two up for way too long, so here's the distinction that finally stuck for me: debounce waits until things go quiet, throttle just limits how often something can fire.

```js
function throttle(fn, limit) {
  let waiting = false;
  return (...args) => {
    if (!waiting) {
      fn(...args);
      waiting = true;
      setTimeout(() => (waiting = false), limit);
    }
  };
}
```

Debounce for search inputs, throttle for scroll or resize handlers. That's the rule of thumb I use now.

Callback hell was scary, then .then() chains got confusing fast once you had more than three of them, and then async/await showed up and everything suddenly read top to bottom like normal code.

```js
async function getUser(id) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('User not found');
    return await response.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch user:', err.message);
  }
}
```

It's still a promise under the hood, but my brain can finally follow along.

The first time I saw destructuring in a code review I thought it looked unnecessarily fancy. Now I can't imagine writing JavaScript without it.

```js
const { title, author, category = 'Uncategorized' } = post;
const [first, second, ...rest] = tags;
```

Default values, renaming, nested destructuring, pulling props straight out of a function argument. It's the kind of syntax sugar that actually makes code easier to read, not harder.

Before CSS custom properties, dark mode meant maintaining two nearly-identical stylesheets. Now it's one variable swap.

```css
:root {
  --bg-color: #ffffff;
  --text-color: #1a1a1a;
}

[data-theme='dark'] {
  --bg-color: #121212;
  --text-color: #f5f5f5;
}

body {
  background: var(--bg-color);
  color: var(--text-color);
}
```

Toggle a data attribute on the html tag with a tiny bit of JS and the whole theme flips instantly.

This used to be the joke that every developer knew, nobody could reliably center a div. Turns out we just needed better tools.

```css
.center {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  height: 100vh;
}
```

Three lines and it's centered both ways, every time. I genuinely don't understand how we survived on table layouts and negative margins.

My first attempt at a CSS animation looked like a strobe light. Turns out easing and duration matter a lot more than I gave them credit for.

```css
@keyframes fadeInUp {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(12px);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
  }
}

.card {
  animation: fadeInUp 0.3s ease-out;
}
```

Subtle, short, and it makes the whole page feel more polished without anyone consciously noticing why.

Media queries respond to the viewport, but components rarely care about the whole viewport, they care about the space they've actually been given.

```css
.card-container {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

@container (min-width: 400px) {
  .card {
    flex-direction: row;
  }
}
```

Now a card can lay itself out differently depending on whether it's in a narrow sidebar or a wide main column, without caring what the browser window is doing.

Old habits die hard, and this is the one line of jQuery that's permanently burned into my fingers from years of maintaining legacy admin panels.

```js
$(document).ready(function () {
  $('.dropdown-toggle').on('click', function () {
    $(this).next('.dropdown-menu').toggleClass('open');
  });
});
```

Modern frameworks have better patterns for this, obviously, but for a quick fix on a decade-old codebase, this still gets the job done in under a minute.

Our internal tools dashboard still runs on jQuery and honestly, for what it does, it doesn't need a rewrite. Toggling a sidebar is still this satisfying.

```js
$('#sidebar-toggle').click(function () {
  $('#sidebar').toggleClass('collapsed');
  $(this).toggleClass('active');
});
```

No virtual DOM, no state management, just a class flipping on click. Sometimes the simple tool really is the right tool.

There's a specific kind of joy in chaining five jQuery methods on one line, and a specific kind of regret six months later when you have to debug it.

```js
$('.alert')
  .fadeIn(200)
  .delay(3000)
  .fadeOut(200, function () {
    $(this).remove();
  });
```

It reads beautifully the day you write it. Whether future-you agrees is a separate question entirely.
